proposer.rationale:
**Hypothesis.** Below the knee (s1.0), the parent trails the bar on P99 TTFT by about 20% in every scenario. On the other tails it is at or better than the bar. A ratio of 1.2 costs about 0.045 fitness per scenario, so it is the largest loss.

The cause is that the parent's decode-protective surcharges run at full strength on an idle cluster:
- the occupancy part of the miss price λ;
- the batch price;
- the interference surcharge;
- the stall floor;
- the decode-context price, which is quadratic in excess resident KV.

Together these over-reward cache affinity and KV balance. They push requests onto replicas that already have queued prefill, which is what the TTFT tail is made of when there is no congestion. Inspiration 1, the best so far, already scales the first four by a congestion gate g. It still leaves the decode-context price ungated. That term is 1.5·excess²/(mean+16k). With a 7k-token median prompt and replicas differing by about 30k resident tokens, it adds about 10k token-equivalents. That is as large as a full queued prompt, and it is unrelated to the request's time to first token.

My change gates the decode-context price by g as well. Below the knee the cost then reduces to roughly the Mooncake min-TTFT estimate: pending prefill, plus the decode budget, plus uncached tokens with in-flight prefixes counted as cached. At the knee the ramp saturates, g = 1, and behaviour is unchanged. The knee is where the parent and Inspiration 1 are already 10–20% better than the bar on TBT and TTFT.

This has not been run through the simulator. The main risk is a small regression in s1.0 TBT and TPOT, which currently sit near 1.0.

**Hypothesis.** The parent's losses come almost entirely from P99 TTFT below the knee: about 1.19–1.21× the bar at s1.0, where the Mooncake min‑TTFT router sets the bar. At the knee it is already ahead of the bar. Below the knee, several terms make an idle, cold replica look more expensive than a warm replica with a backlog:

- the miss price λ, which is around 3 at moderate occupancy;
- the always‑on decode‑stall floor;
- the batch and context guards.

So the parent sends requests behind queued prefill work, and they wait long enough to set the TTFT tail. Removing the imbalance guards in this ablation took away the only brake on that.

The fix is a **TTFT‑feasibility filter**. For each replica, estimate time to first token the way Mooncake does: pending prefill plus this request's uncached tokens, counting in‑flight prefixes as cached. A replica is eligible only if its estimate is within a slack of the best one. The slack is a fraction of the larger of the best estimate and the prompt length, plus a fixed floor. It widens as 1/(1 − ramp), using the parent's existing congestion ramp. Below the knee (ramp ≈ 0), no request waits much longer than the Mooncake choice, which should bring TTFT back to the bar. At the knee (ramp → 1) the filter disappears and the parent's TBT/TPOT/E2E gains are untouched. Within the eligible set the parent's full cost model still decides, so cache affinity and decode smoothness are kept where they cost little TTFT. None of this has been run through the simulator yet; the slack constants are untested.
